            flashcards: [
                { front: "What is Ohm's Law and its mathematical formula?", back: "\\(V = IR\\). Potential difference (\\(V\\)) across a conductor is directly proportional to the current (\\(I\\)) flowing through it, where \\(R\\) is resistance in Ohms (\\(\\Omega\\))." },
                { front: "What are the four core formulas for electrical power (P)?", back: "\\(P = \\frac{W}{t} = \\frac{E}{t}\\), \\(P = VI\\), \\(P = I^2 R\\), and \\(P = \\frac{V^2}{R}\\). Its SI unit is the Watt (\\(1\\,\\mathrm{W} = 1\\,\\mathrm{J/s}\\))." },
                { front: "How do you rearrange \\(P = VI\\) to find current and voltage?", back: "\\(I = \\frac{P}{V}\\) and \\(V = \\frac{P}{I}\\)." },
                { front: "How do you rearrange \\(P = I^2 R\\) to find current and resistance?", back: "\\(I = \\sqrt{\\frac{P}{R}}\\) and \\(R = \\frac{P}{I^2}\\)." },
                { front: "How do you rearrange \\(P = \\frac{V^2}{R}\\) to find resistance and voltage?", back: "\\(R = \\frac{V^2}{P}\\) and \\(V = \\sqrt{P \\times R}\\)." },
                { front: "What is the formula for electrical energy in terms of electric charge?", back: "\\(E = QV\\), where \\(E\\) is energy in Joules (J), \\(Q\\) is charge in Coulombs (C), and \\(V\\) is potential difference in Volts (V)." },
                { front: "What are the formulas for total electrical energy transferred over time (t)?", back: "\\(E = Pt = VIt = I^2 Rt = \\frac{V^2}{R}t\\). Time must be in seconds (s) to obtain energy in Joules (J)." },
                { front: "What is Specific Heat Capacity (c) and what is its SI unit?", back: "The heat energy required to raise the temperature of 1 kg of a substance by 1°C: \\(c = \\frac{Q}{m\\Delta T}\\). Its unit is \\(\\mathrm{J/(kg\\cdot^\\circ C)}\\)." },
                { front: "What is the formula for thermal heat absorbed or released by a substance?", back: "\\(Q = mc\\Delta T\\), where \\(m\\) is mass in kg, \\(c\\) is specific heat capacity, and \\(\\Delta T\\) is the temperature change in °C." },
                { front: "State the equation for Conservation of Energy in Joule Heating.", back: "\\(RI^2 t = mc\\Delta T\\): electrical energy dissipated by a resistor in an insulated calorimeter equals the heat absorbed by the liquid." },
                { front: "How is energy distributed in an electric lighting device?", back: "\\(E = E_L + E_H\\), where \\(E_L = E \\times (\\%\\text{ of light})\\) and \\(E_H = E \\times (\\%\\text{ of heat})\\)." },
                { front: "Why are traditional incandescent filament bulbs considered energy-inefficient?", back: "Only about 10% of the input electrical energy is converted into useful visible light; the remaining 90% is wasted as unwanted thermal heat." },
                { front: "What is the commercial unit of electricity and how many Joules does it equal?", back: "The kilowatt-hour (kWh). \\(1\\,\\mathrm{kWh} = 1000\\,\\mathrm{W} \\times 3600\\,\\mathrm{s} = 3.6 \\times 10^6\\,\\mathrm{J} = 3.6\\,\\mathrm{MJ}\\)." },
                { front: "What is the formula for calculating electricity bills?", back: "\\(\\text{Cost} = E(\\text{kWh}) \\times \\text{unit price per kWh}\\), where \\(E(\\text{kWh}) = \\frac{P(\\text{W}) \\times t(\\text{h})}{1000}\\)." },
                { front: "What is electrical resistance (R) and what factors affect it?", back: "Opposition to electric charge flow: \\(R = \\frac{V}{I}\\). It depends on wire length (\\(R \\propto l\\)), cross-sectional area (\\(R \\propto 1/A\\)), material resistivity, and temperature." },
                { front: "Why does conductor resistance increase as temperature rises?", back: "Higher temperature causes metal ions in the lattice to vibrate more violently, increasing collisions with passing conduction electrons." },
                { front: "What is Joule's Law of Electric Heating?", back: "The rate of heat dissipation in a resistor is directly proportional to resistance and to the square of the current: \\(Q = I^2 R t\\)." },
                { front: "Calculation: What is the resistance of a 240 V, 1200 W heater?", back: "\\(R = \\frac{V^2}{P} = \\frac{(240)^2}{1200} = \\frac{57{,}600}{1200} = 48\\,\\Omega\\)." },
                { front: "Calculation: A 2000 W heater runs for 3 hours. How many kWh are consumed?", back: "\\(E = \\frac{2000 \\times 3}{1000} = 6.0\\,\\mathrm{kWh}\\)." },
                { front: "Calculation: 50 C of charge moves across a 12 V potential difference. What work is done?", back: "\\(E = QV = 50\\,\\mathrm{C} \\times 12\\,\\mathrm{V} = 600\\,\\mathrm{J}\\)." },
                { front: "Common Mistake: Using minutes or hours in \\(E = Pt\\) to calculate Joules.", back: "Remember: \\(E = Pt\\) gives Joules ONLY when \\(t\\) is in SECONDS. If using hours, you calculate kilowatt-hours (if \\(P\\) is in kW)." },
                { front: "Common Mistake: Forgetting to square current in \\(P = I^2 R\\).", back: "Do not calculate \\((I \\times R)^2\\) or \\(I \\times R\\). You must square current \\(I\\) FIRST, then multiply by resistance \\(R\\)." }
            ],
