            flashcards: [
                { front: "What does the fundamental Law of Electrostatics state?", back: "Like electric charges repel each other; unlike (opposite) electric charges attract each other." },
                { front: "What are the two types of electric charges and their subatomic carriers?", back: "Positive charge (carried by protons in atomic nuclei) and Negative charge (carried by orbiting electrons). Neutrons have zero electric charge." },
                { front: "What is the SI unit of electric charge?", back: "The Coulomb (symbol C). 1 Coulomb equals the combined charge of \\( 6.25 \\times 10^{18} \\) electrons." },
                { front: "What is the elementary unit of electric charge \\( e \\)?", back: "\\( e = 1.6 \\times 10^{-19}\\,\\mathrm{C} \\). An electron carries \\( -1.6 \\times 10^{-19}\\,\\mathrm{C} \\) and a proton carries \\( +1.6 \\times 10^{-19}\\,\\mathrm{C} \\)." },
                { front: "What is the formula for electric charge quantization?", back: "\\( Q = N \\times e \\), where \\( N \\) is the integer number of elementary charges." },
                { front: "How do you rearrange the quantization equation to find the number of electrons?", back: "\\( N = \\frac{Q}{e} \\)" },
                { front: "How does friction charge polythene and glass rods?", back: "Rubbing transfers electrons: Polythene rod gains electrons from wool to become negative. Glass rod loses electrons to silk to become positive." },
                { front: "What is electrostatic induction?", back: "The redistribution of electric charges on a neutral conductor caused by the nearby presence of an external charged object without physical contact." },
                { front: "State Coulomb's Law of Electrostatic Force mathematically.", back: "\\( F = k \\frac{|q_1 q_2|}{r^2} \\), where \\( k \\approx 8.99 \\times 10^9\\,\\mathrm{N\\cdot m^2/C^2} \\) (or \\( 9.0 \\times 10^9 \\))." },
                { front: "What happens to the electrostatic force between two charges if the separation distance is doubled?", back: "The force decreases to one-quarter (\\( \\frac{1}{4} \\)) of its initial value, because electrostatic force obeys an inverse-square law (\\( F \\propto \\frac{1}{r^2} \\))." },
                { front: "What is electric field strength and its formula?", back: "The electrostatic force experienced per unit positive test charge placed at a point: \\( E = \\frac{F}{q} \\)." },
                { front: "What are the SI units of electric field strength?", back: "Newton per Coulomb (\\( \\mathrm{N/C} \\)) or Volt per meter (\\( \\mathrm{V/m} \\))." },
                { front: "How do you calculate the electrostatic force acting on a charge in an electric field?", back: "\\( F = qE \\)" },
                { front: "What is the convention for the direction of electric field lines?", back: "Electric field lines point away from positive charges and point toward negative charges (direction of force on a positive test charge)." },
                { front: "What is the relationship between electric potential difference, work done, and charge?", back: "\\( V = \\frac{W}{q} \\iff W = qV \\iff q = \\frac{W}{V} \\)" },
                { front: "What is 1 Volt defined as in terms of basic SI units?", back: "\\( 1\\,\\mathrm{Volt} = 1\\,\\mathrm{Joule/Coulomb} \\; (1\\,\\mathrm{V} = 1\\,\\mathrm{J/C}) \\)." },
                { front: "What are the three primary uses of a gold-leaf electroscope?", back: "1. Detect the presence of electric charge. 2. Determine the sign (+ or -) of charge. 3. Test whether a material is a conductor or insulator." },
                { front: "Why do the leaves of an electroscope diverge when charged?", back: "Like charges spread over the brass rod and the thin gold leaf, causing mutual electrostatic repulsion that pushes the leaf outward." },
                { front: "What is action at sharp points (corona discharge)?", back: "Charge concentrates intensely at sharp points, producing a strong localized electric field that ionizes surrounding air and allows charge to leak away." },
                { front: "How do lightning conductors protect tall structures?", back: "A pointed copper rod atop the building safely bleeds off induced cloud charges via corona discharge and provides a low-resistance path to earth if struck." },
                { front: "Why must aircraft and fuel tankers be electrically grounded before refueling?", back: "Friction with air or hoses generates large static charges. Grounding discharges the vehicle to earth, preventing dangerous sparks that could ignite fuel vapors." }
            ],
