            flashcards: [
                { front: "What is the fundamental difference between speed and velocity?", back: "Speed is a scalar quantity measuring distance travelled per unit time regardless of direction. Velocity is a vector quantity measuring displacement per unit time in a specified direction." },
                { front: "What is the SI unit of speed and velocity?", back: "Meter per second (\\( \\mathrm{m/s} \\))." },
                { front: "What is the defining formula for average speed?", back: "\\( v_{\\text{avg}} = \\frac{\\text{Total Distance}}{\\text{Total Time}} = \\frac{d_{\\text{total}}}{t_{\\text{total}}} \\)" },
                { front: "What is a common student misconception regarding average speed?", back: "Averaging speeds directly as \\( \\frac{v_1 + v_2}{2} \\) is incorrect if the time spent at each speed is different. Always divide total distance by total time." },
                { front: "What is acceleration and its defining mathematical formula?", back: "Acceleration is the rate of change of velocity with time: \\( a = \\frac{v - u}{t} \\), where \\( u \\) is initial velocity and \\( v \\) is final velocity." },
                { front: "What is the SI unit of acceleration?", back: "Meter per second squared (\\( \\mathrm{m/s^2} \\))." },
                { front: "How do you rearrange the acceleration formula to find final velocity?", back: "\\( v = u + at \\)" },
                { front: "How do you rearrange the acceleration formula to find the time taken?", back: "\\( t = \\frac{v - u}{a} \\)" },
                { front: "What is deceleration (retardation)?", back: "Negative acceleration that occurs when an object's speed decreases over time (\\( v < u \\implies a < 0 \\))." },
                { front: "How do you convert speed from km/h to m/s?", back: "Divide by 3.6 (e.g. \\( 90\\,\\mathrm{km/h} \\div 3.6 = 25\\,\\mathrm{m/s} \\))." },
                { front: "How do you convert speed from m/s to km/h?", back: "Multiply by 3.6 (e.g. \\( 20\\,\\mathrm{m/s} \\times 3.6 = 72\\,\\mathrm{km/h} \\))." },
                { front: "What is the relative speed formula for two objects moving towards each other (opposite directions)?", back: "\\( v_{\\text{rel}} = v_1 + v_2 \\) (their closing speed is the sum of their speeds)." },
                { front: "What is the relative speed formula for two objects moving in the same direction?", back: "\\( v_{\\text{rel}} = |v_1 - v_2| \\) (their separation/overtaking speed is the difference between their speeds)." },
                { front: "What is the formula for distance covered under uniform acceleration using average velocity?", back: "\\( d = v_{\\text{avg}} \\times t = \\left(\\frac{u + v}{2}\\right)t \\)" },
                { front: "In a 50 Hz tickertape timer, what is the time interval of 1 tick?", back: "1 tick = \\( \\frac{1}{50}\\,\\mathrm{s} = 0.02\\,\\mathrm{s} \\)." },
                { front: "What is a 'tentick' in tickertape timer analysis?", back: "A tentick is the time interval spanning 10 dots: \\( 10 \\times 0.02\\,\\mathrm{s} = 0.2\\,\\mathrm{s} \\)." },
                { front: "How do tickertape strips indicate uniform velocity vs uniform acceleration?", back: "Strips of equal length indicate uniform velocity. Strips whose lengths increase in equal steps indicate uniform acceleration." },
                { front: "What is the difference between instantaneous speed and average speed?", back: "Instantaneous speed is the speed of an object at an exact single instant (shown on a car speedometer), while average speed is total distance divided by total trip time." },
                { front: "How does a photogate measure speed in a physics laboratory?", back: "A photogate measures the time \\( \\Delta t \\) that an interrupt card of length \\( L \\) blocks an infrared light beam: \\( v = \\frac{L}{\\Delta t} \\)." },
                { front: "Can an object have zero velocity but non-zero acceleration?", back: "Yes. At the highest point of its vertical flight under gravity, a thrown ball has instantaneous velocity \\( v = 0 \\), but acceleration is \\( g = 9.8\\,\\mathrm{m/s^2} \\) downwards." }
            ],
