                { front: "State the Third Equation of Motion.", back: "\\( s = ut + \\frac{1}{2}at^2 \\), which calculates displacement without requiring final velocity." },
                { front: "For an object accelerating from rest (u = 0), how does the third equation simplify?", back: "\\( s = \\frac{1}{2}at^2 \\)" },
                { front: "State the Fourth Equation of Motion.", back: "\\( v^2 = u^2 + 2as \\iff s = \\frac{v^2 - u^2}{2a} \\), independent of time \\( t \\)." },
                { front: "Why does doubling the initial speed quadruple the emergency braking distance?", back: "Because \\( v^2 = u^2 + 2as \\implies s \\propto u^2 \\). Doubling speed multiplies braking distance by \\( 2^2 = 4 \\)." },
                { front: "What is the formula for displacement during the n-th individual second of motion?", back: "\\( s_n = u + \\frac{1}{2}a(2n - 1) \\)" },
                { front: "What is Galileo's rule of odd numbers for motion from rest under constant acceleration?", back: "Displacements during successive equal time intervals are in the ratio of consecutive odd numbers: \\( 1 : 3 : 5 : 7 : \\dots \\)." },
                { front: "What is the formula for average velocity in uniformly accelerated motion?", back: "\\( v_{\\text{avg}} = \\frac{u + v}{2} \\)" },
                { front: "How is the area under a trapezoidal v-t graph split for calculation?", back: "Into a lower rectangle of area \\( ut \\) and an upper triangle of area \\( \\frac{1}{2}(v - u)t = \\frac{1}{2}at^2 \\), giving \\( s = ut + \\frac{1}{2}at^2 \\)." },
                { front: "What does a negative gradient on a velocity-time graph represent?", back: "Deceleration (retardation), indicating that velocity is decreasing." },
                { front: "What does area below the time axis on a velocity-time graph represent?", back: "Negative displacement, indicating that the object has reversed direction and is moving backwards." },
                { front: "What strategy should you use to pick the correct kinematic equation?", back: "Identify the 3 given variables and the 1 required variable, then pick the equation that contains only those 4 variables." },
                { front: "How does terminal velocity appear on a velocity-time graph?", back: "A curve whose slope progressively decreases and asymptotically levels off to a horizontal line as air resistance balances weight." },
                { front: "What is the primary unit consistency rule when using kinematic equations?", back: "All distances must be in meters (m), velocities in m/s, times in seconds (s), and accelerations in m/s². Never mix km/h with seconds directly." },
                { front: "What does a steep straight line on an s-t graph represent compared to a gentle straight line?", back: "A steeper slope represents a greater velocity, while a gentler slope represents a slower velocity." }
