                    feedback: "<strong>Step-by-step Solution:</strong><br>1. <strong>Given:</strong> Mass \\( m = 10{,}000\\,\\mathrm{kg} \\), Height \\( h = 80.0\\,\\mathrm{m} \\), \\( g = 9.80\\,\\mathrm{N/kg} \\).<br>2. <strong>Find:</strong> Gravitational potential energy \\( \\Delta E_p \\).<br>3. <strong>Equation:</strong> \\( \\Delta E_p = mgh \\).<br>4. <strong>Unit conversion:</strong> Standard SI units.<br>5. <strong>Rearrangement:</strong> Direct calculation.<br>6. <strong>Substitution:</strong> \\( \\Delta E_p = 10{,}000 \\times 9.80 \\times 80.0 \\).<br>7. <strong>Calculation:</strong> \\( 98{,}000 \\times 80.0 = 7{,}840{,}000\\,\\mathrm{J} = 7.84\\,\\mathrm{MJ} \\).<br>8. <strong>Final Answer:</strong> \\( 7.84\\,\\mathrm{MJ} \\).<br>9. <strong>Explanation:</strong> The stored water contains 7.84 Megajoules of potential energy ready for electricity generation."
