                    feedback: "<strong>Step-by-step Solution:</strong><br>1. <strong>Given:</strong> Mass \\( m = 2000\\,\\mathrm{kg} \\), Initial velocity \\( u = 10.0\\,\\mathrm{m/s} \\), Final velocity \\( v = 30.0\\,\\mathrm{m/s} \\).<br>2. <strong>Find:</strong> Net work \\( W_{\\text{net}} \\).<br>3. <strong>Equation:</strong> \\( W_{\\text{net}} = \\frac{1}{2}m(v^2 - u^2) \\).<br>4. <strong>Unit conversion:</strong> Standard SI units.<br>5. <strong>Rearrangement:</strong> Direct calculation.<br>6. <strong>Substitution:</strong> \\( W_{\\text{net}} = \\frac{1}{2}(2000)(30.0^2 - 10.0^2) \\).<br>7. <strong>Calculation:</strong> \\( 30.0^2 - 10.0^2 = 900 - 100 = 800 \\); \\( 1000 \\times 800 = 800{,}000\\,\\mathrm{J} = 800\\,\\mathrm{kJ} \\).<br>8. <strong>Final Answer:</strong> \\( 800\\,\\mathrm{kJ} \\).<br>9. <strong>Explanation:</strong> The work done on the car equals its gain in kinetic energy (800 kJ)."
