            flashcards: [
                { front: "What is thermal conduction?", back: "The transfer of thermal energy (heat) through matter from regions of higher to lower temperature without bulk movement of the matter as a whole." },
                { front: "Why are metals vastly superior conductors of heat compared to non-metals?", back: "Metals contain dense populations of free mobile electrons that drift rapidly through the lattice, transferring kinetic energy to cooler atoms." },
                { front: "How does heat conduct through non-metals (insulators)?", back: "By slow acoustic lattice vibrations where vibrating atoms jostle neighbouring atoms, without any assisting mobile free electrons." },
                { front: "State Fourier's Law of Thermal Conduction.", back: "The rate of heat flow is directly proportional to thermal conductivity \\( k \\), surface area \\( A \\), and temperature difference \\( \\Delta T \\), and inversely proportional to thickness \\( d \\): \\( \\frac{Q}{t} = k A \\frac{\\Delta T}{d} \\)." },
                { front: "What are the SI units of thermal conductivity \\( k \\)?", back: "Watts per meter Kelvin: \\( \\mathrm{W/(m\\cdot K)} \\) (or \\( \\mathrm{W/(m\\cdot ^\\circ C)} \\))." },
                { front: "What is thermal resistance (\\( R_{\\text{th}} \\)) and how is it calculated?", back: "It measures opposition to conductive heat flow: \\( R_{\\text{th}} = \\frac{d}{kA} \\). Heat flow rate is \\( \\frac{Q}{t} = \\frac{\\Delta T}{R_{\\text{th}}} \\). SI unit: \\( \\mathrm{K/W} \\)." },
                { front: "What is thermal convection?", back: "The transfer of thermal energy through fluids (liquids and gases) by the bulk movement of the heated fluid itself, driven by buoyant density differences." },
                { front: "What drives convection currents in a fluid?", back: "When heated, fluid expands and becomes less dense; cooler, denser surrounding fluid sinks beneath it, pushing warmer fluid upward: \\( \\Delta \\rho = \\rho_{\\text{cold}} - \\rho_{\\text{hot}} \\)." },
                { front: "Why can convection never occur in solids?", back: "Because atoms in solids are locked into fixed positions within a rigid lattice and cannot flow or circulate in bulk." },
                { front: "Why is trapped stationary air one of the best thermal insulators?", back: "Still air has an exceptionally low thermal conductivity (\\( k \\approx 0.026\\,\\mathrm{W/(m\\cdot K)} \\)), and trapping it in tiny pockets prevents convection currents." },
                { front: "Why is the heating element placed at the bottom of an electric kettle or water cylinder?", back: "So that heated, less dense water at the bottom immediately rises by convection, establishing a continuous circular current that heats the entire volume evenly." },
                { front: "How does a daytime sea breeze form along the coastline?", back: "Sunlight warms land faster than water. Warm air over land expands and rises, and cooler, denser air blows in from the sea to replace it." },
                { front: "How does a nighttime land breeze form?", back: "At night, land cools faster than the sea. Warmer air over the sea rises, drawing a cool breeze outward from the land toward the water." },
                { front: "Which common metal is the fastest conductor of heat?", back: "Copper, followed closely by aluminium." },
                { front: "Why do cooking pans have metal bases but wooden or plastic handles?", back: "The copper/aluminium base conducts heat quickly to the food, while the plastic/wood handle has low conductivity and remains safe to touch." },
                { front: "How does cavity wall insulation reduce heat loss in a home?", back: "It fills the empty air cavity between brick walls with insulating foam or mineral wool, eliminating convection loops and reducing conduction." },
                { front: "How does double glazing reduce window heat loss?", back: "Trapping a thin layer of dry air or argon gas between two glass panes provides high thermal resistance, dramatically slowing conductive and convective heat transfer." },
                { front: "If a wall's thickness \\( d \\) is doubled, what happens to its rate of heat conduction?", back: "The rate of heat conduction is halved (\\( \\frac{Q}{t} \\propto \\frac{1}{d} \\))." },
                { front: "If the temperature difference \\( \\Delta T \\) across a window triples, what happens to the heat loss rate?", back: "The rate of heat loss triples (\\( \\frac{Q}{t} \\propto \\Delta T \\))." },
                { front: "Why do birds fluff their feathers and animals raise their fur in cold weather?", back: "Fluffing traps a thicker layer of stagnant air next to the skin, increasing thermal insulation." },
                { front: "What is the temperature gradient across a conducting wall?", back: "The temperature drop per unit thickness: \\( \\frac{\\Delta T}{d} \\), measured in Kelvin per meter (\\( \\mathrm{K/m} \\))." },
                { front: "How does loft insulation reduce building heating costs?", back: "Warm air rises to the ceiling by convection; thick fibreglass insulation laid on the attic floor provides high thermal resistance, halting heat loss through the roof." }
            ],
