                    feedback: "<strong>Step-by-step Solution:</strong><br>1. <strong>Given:</strong> \\( \\varepsilon = 1.0 \\), \\( A = 0.50\\,\\mathrm{m^2} \\), \\( T = 500\\,\\mathrm{K} \\), \\( \\sigma = 5.67 \\times 10^{-8}\\,\\mathrm{W/(m^2\\cdot K^4)} \\).<br>2. <strong>Find:</strong> Radiated power \\( P \\).<br>3. <strong>Equation:</strong> \\( P = \\varepsilon \\sigma A T^4 \\).<br>4. <strong>Unit conversion:</strong> Standard SI units.<br>5. <strong>Rearrangement:</strong> Direct calculation.<br>6. <strong>Substitution:</strong> \\( T^4 = 500^4 = 6.25 \\times 10^{10} \\); \\( P = 1.0 \\times (5.67 \\times 10^{-8}) \\times 0.50 \\times (6.25 \\times 10^{10}) \\).<br>7. <strong>Calculation:</strong> \\( P \\approx 1771.9\\,\\mathrm{W} \\approx 1.77\\,\\mathrm{kW} \\).<br>8. <strong>Final Answer:</strong> \\( 1.77\\,\\mathrm{kW} \\) (or 1772 W).<br>9. <strong>Explanation:</strong> Radiant power scales with the fourth power of temperature, producing 1.77 kW."
