            flashcards: [
                { front: "State Ohm's Law.", back: "The current through a metallic conductor is directly proportional to the potential difference across it, provided temperature and physical conditions remain constant: \\( V = IR \\)." },
                { front: "What are the formula rearrangements of Ohm's Law?", back: "Current: \\( I = \\frac{V}{R} \\); Resistance: \\( R = \\frac{V}{I} \\); Voltage: \\( V = IR \\)." },
                { front: "How is electric current distributed in a series circuit?", back: "Current is identical at every point throughout the single closed loop: \\( I = I_1 = I_2 = I_3 \\)." },
                { front: "How is potential difference distributed in a series circuit?", back: "The total source voltage equals the sum of potential differences across the individual components: \\( V = V_1 + V_2 + V_3 \\)." },
                { front: "What is the total equivalent resistance of resistors in series?", back: "\\( R_{\\text{tot}} = R_1 + R_2 + R_3 + \\dots \\) (individual resistances add directly together)." },
                { front: "How does potential difference behave across parallel branches?", back: "The potential difference across every parallel branch is identical and equals the full supply voltage: \\( V = V_1 = V_2 \\)." },
                { front: "How does electric current behave in a parallel circuit?", back: "The total current drawn from the supply equals the sum of the currents passing through the individual branches: \\( I_{\\text{tot}} = I_1 + I_2 + \\dots \\)." },
                { front: "What is the equivalent resistance formula for two resistors in parallel?", back: "Product over sum: \\( R_p = \\frac{R_1 R_2}{R_1 + R_2} \\) (or \\( \\frac{1}{R_p} = \\frac{1}{R_1} + \\frac{1}{R_2} \\))." },
                { front: "What happens to the total circuit resistance when an additional resistor is added in parallel?", back: "The total equivalent resistance decreases, because adding another branch provides an additional path for electric charge to flow." },
                { front: "What is electrical resistivity (\\( \\rho \\)) and its formula?", back: "An intrinsic property of a conducting material measuring opposition to electric current: \\( R = \\rho \\frac{L}{A} \\iff \\rho = \\frac{RA}{L} \\). SI unit: Ohm meter (\\( \\Omega\\cdot\\mathrm{m} \\))." },
                { front: "How does the electrical resistance of a wire change if its length is doubled?", back: "The resistance doubles (\\( R \\propto L \\))." },
                { front: "How does the electrical resistance of a wire change if its cross-sectional area is doubled?", back: "The resistance is halved (\\( R \\propto \\frac{1}{A} \\))." },
                { front: "What is electromotive force (EMF)?", back: "The total electrical energy supplied by a source per coulomb of charge that passes through it: \\( E = \\frac{W}{Q} \\), measured in Volts (V)." },
                { front: "What are 'lost volts' in a real DC power source?", back: "The internal potential drop across the battery's internal resistance \\( r \\) when driving current: \\( V_{\\text{lost}} = Ir \\)." },
                { front: "What is terminal potential difference and its equation?", back: "The actual voltage available across the external battery terminals: \\( V = E - Ir = IR \\)." },
                { front: "What is a potential divider circuit?", back: "A series arrangement of two or more resistors that divides input voltage into an output voltage proportional to resistance: \\( V_{\\text{out}} = V_{\\text{in}} \\left(\\frac{R_2}{R_1 + R_2}\\right) \\)." },
                { front: "How does a Light-Dependent Resistor (LDR) behave in a potential divider?", back: "In darkness, LDR resistance is very high, producing a large voltage drop; in bright light, resistance plunges, dropping the voltage across it." },
                { front: "How does an NTC thermistor behave in a temperature-sensing circuit?", back: "As temperature increases, its resistance decreases, reducing the voltage drop across it in a potential divider." },
                { front: "How must an ammeter be connected in an electric circuit?", back: "Always in series with the component, because an ideal ammeter has negligible (zero) resistance and must measure all current flowing." },
                { front: "How must a voltmeter be connected in an electric circuit?", back: "Always in parallel across the component, because an ideal voltmeter has infinite resistance to avoid diverting current from the circuit." },
                { front: "What happens in a series circuit if one lamp blows?", back: "The circuit is broken at that point and all lamps in the chain go out immediately." },
                { front: "Why are domestic lights and appliances connected in parallel?", back: "So each appliance receives full mains voltage (230 V) and operates independently without turning off other devices if switched off or damaged." }
            ],
