            flashcards: [
                { front: "What is Electrical Power (P) and what is its SI unit?", back: "Electrical power is the rate at which electrical energy is transferred or converted into other energy forms. Its SI unit is the Watt (W), where \\(1\\,\\mathrm{W} = 1\\,\\mathrm{J/s}\\)." },
                { front: "What are the three core formulas for electrical power?", back: "\\(P = IV\\), \\(P = I^2 R\\), and \\(P = \\frac{V^2}{R}\\)." },
                { front: "State the symbols and SI units in \\(P = IV\\).", back: "\\(P\\) = electrical power in Watts (\\(\\mathrm{W}\\)), \\(I\\) = electric current in Amperes (\\(\\mathrm{A}\\)), and \\(V\\) = potential difference in Volts (\\(\\mathrm{V}\\))." },
                { front: "How do you rearrange \\(P = IV\\) to solve for current \\(I\\) and voltage \\(V\\)?", back: "\\(I = \\frac{P}{V}\\) and \\(V = \\frac{P}{I}\\)." },
                { front: "When is \\(P = I^2 R\\) most convenient to use?", back: "When electric current \\(I\\) and resistance \\(R\\) are known, such as calculating Joule heat dissipation in series resistors or transmission cables." },
                { front: "When is \\(P = \\frac{V^2}{R}\\) most convenient to use?", back: "When potential difference \\(V\\) and resistance \\(R\\) are known, especially for parallel domestic appliances connected to a constant mains voltage (e.g., 230 V)." },
                { front: "What is the formula for electrical energy transferred (\\(E\\)) in Joules?", back: "\\(E = Pt = VIt = I^2 Rt = \\frac{V^2}{R}t\\), where \\(E\\) is energy in Joules (J) when time \\(t\\) is in seconds (s)." },
                { front: "What is a kilowatt-hour (kWh) and how many Joules does it equal?", back: "The kilowatt-hour is the commercial unit of electrical energy. \\(1\\,\\mathrm{kWh} = 1000\\,\\mathrm{W} \\times 3600\\,\\mathrm{s} = 3.6 \\times 10^6\\,\\mathrm{J} = 3.6\\,\\mathrm{MJ}\\)." },
                { front: "How do you calculate the commercial cost of electricity?", back: "\\(\\text{Cost} = E_{(\\mathrm{kWh})} \\times \\text{Unit Tariff}\\), where \\(E_{(\\mathrm{kWh})} = P_{(\\mathrm{kW})} \\times t_{(\\mathrm{h})}\\)." },
                { front: "What is Joule's Law of Electric Heating?", back: "\\(Q = I^2 R t\\): the thermal heat energy \\(Q\\) produced in a resistor is directly proportional to resistance \\(R\\), duration \\(t\\), and the square of current (\\(I^2\\))." },
                { front: "Why is electricity transmitted across long distances at very high voltages?", back: "From \\(P = IV\\), stepping up voltage dramatically lowers current \\(I\\). Since Joule losses equal \\(P_{\\text{loss}} = I^2 R\\), reducing current drastically minimizes cable heat losses." },
                { front: "How is the normal operating current of a domestic appliance calculated?", back: "\\(I = \\frac{P}{V}\\), dividing rated power in Watts by mains supply voltage (typically 230 V)." },
                { front: "What is a fuse and what is its working principle?", back: "A safety device with a thin wire of low melting point that heats up, melts, and breaks the circuit when current exceeds its rated limit." },
                { front: "How is a suitable fuse rating chosen for a domestic appliance?", back: "Choose the standard rating (e.g., 3 A, 5 A, or 13 A) immediately above the normal operating current \\(I = \\frac{P}{V}\\)." },
                { front: "Why must switches and fuses ALWAYS be placed in the Live wire?", back: "Placing them in the live wire isolates the appliance from the dangerous 230 V supply when turned off or when the fuse blows, eliminating electric shock hazards." },
                { front: "What are the international insulation colors in domestic mains wiring?", back: "Live wire = Brown; Neutral wire = Blue; Earth wire = Green and Yellow stripes." },
                { front: "What is the role of the Earth wire in a 3-pin plug?", back: "It connects the metal appliance case to ground (0 V). If a fault makes the case live, a huge current flows to earth, immediately blowing the fuse and cutting power." },
                { front: "What does 'double insulation' mean, and what is its symbol?", back: "Appliances with two independent layers of insulation around live components (marked with a square within a square symbol). They do not require an earth wire." },
                { front: "What is a Residual Current Device (RCD / RCCB)?", back: "An electromagnetic safety switch that detects any difference between live and neutral currents (leakage to earth) and trips in milliseconds, protecting humans from lethal shocks." },
                { front: "Calculation: What is the operating current and resistance of a 230 V, 920 W toaster?", back: "Current \\(I = \\frac{920}{230} = 4.0\\,\\mathrm{A}\\); Resistance \\(R = \\frac{V}{I} = \\frac{230}{4} = 57.5\\,\\Omega\\)." },
                { front: "Common Mistake: Calculating energy in Joules using hours.", back: "Remember: \\(E = Pt\\) yields Joules ONLY when \\(t\\) is in SECONDS. If using hours, you calculate kilowatt-hours (\\(\\mathrm{kWh}\\)) if \\(P\\) is in \\(\\mathrm{kW}\\)." },
                { front: "Common Mistake: Thinking doubling current doubles Joule heating.", back: "Incorrect. Because \\(P = I^2 R\\), doubling current quadruples (\\(2^2 = 4\\)) the rate of thermal heat generation." }
            ],
