            flashcards: [
                { front: "What is a wave and what does it transfer?", back: "A wave is a periodic disturbance that transmits energy from one place to another through space or a medium without permanently transferring matter." },
                { front: "What experiment proves that waves transfer energy without transferring matter?", back: "A floating styrofoam ball or toy duck on water ripples bobs up and down vertically in place; it does not travel horizontally across the water with the wave crests." },
                { front: "What is a transverse wave? Give two examples.", back: "A wave in which medium particles oscillate perpendicularly (at right angles) to the direction of wave travel. Examples: water ripples, light waves, and all electromagnetic waves." },
                { front: "What is a longitudinal wave? Give two examples.", back: "A wave in which medium particles oscillate back and forth parallel to the direction of wave propagation. Examples: sound waves in air and compressed slinky springs." },
                { front: "Can sound waves travel through a vacuum?", back: "No; sound waves are mechanical longitudinal waves that require particles of a material medium to collide and propagate." },
                { front: "Can light and electromagnetic waves travel through a vacuum?", back: "Yes; electromagnetic waves consist of oscillating electric and magnetic fields and do not require a material medium, traveling at \\(c = 3.0 \\times 10^8\\,\\mathrm{m/s}\\) in a vacuum." },
                { front: "What is Amplitude (A) and what does it indicate about a wave?", back: "Amplitude is the maximum displacement of a particle from its undisturbed equilibrium (rest) position. Greater amplitude indicates greater wave energy." },
                { front: "What is Wavelength (λ) and what is its SI unit?", back: "The distance between two successive corresponding points in phase on a wave, such as crest-to-crest or compression-to-compression. Measured in meters (m)." },
                { front: "What is Wave Period (T) and what is its SI unit?", back: "The time taken for one complete wave oscillation or cycle to pass a fixed point. Its SI unit is the second (s)." },
                { front: "What is Frequency (f) and what is its SI unit?", back: "The number of complete wave cycles passing a point per second. Measured in Hertz (Hz), where \\(1\\,\\mathrm{Hz} = 1\\,\\mathrm{s^{-1}}\\)." },
                { front: "What is the relationship between Period (T) and Frequency (f)?", back: "\\(T = \\frac{1}{f}\\) and \\(f = \\frac{1}{T}\\). Period and frequency are mathematical reciprocals of each other." },
                { front: "What is the Universal Wave Equation?", back: "\\(v = f \\lambda\\), where \\(v\\) is wave speed (\\(\\mathrm{m/s}\\)), \\(f\\) is frequency (\\(\\mathrm{Hz}\\)), and \\(\\lambda\\) is wavelength (\\(\\mathrm{m}\\))." },
                { front: "How do you rearrange the wave equation \\(v = f \\lambda\\)?", back: "To find frequency: \\(f = \\frac{v}{\\lambda}\\). To find wavelength: \\(\\lambda = \\frac{v}{f}\\)." },
                { front: "What is Angular Frequency (ω) and what is its formula?", back: "The rate of change of wave phase angle in radians per unit time: \\(\\omega = 2\\pi f = \\frac{2\\pi}{T}\\). Its unit is radians per second (rad/s)." },
                { front: "What is Wave Number (k) and what is its formula?", back: "The spatial frequency of a wave indicating radians per meter: \\(k = \\frac{2\\pi}{\\lambda}\\). Its unit is radians per meter (rad/m)." },
                { front: "How is wave propagation speed (v) calculated from angular quantities?", back: "\\(v = \\frac{\\omega}{k}\\), dividing angular frequency (rad/s) by wave number (rad/m)." },
                { front: "What is the formula for calculating echo distance (d)?", back: "\\(d = \\frac{v \\times t}{2}\\) (or \\(2d = v \\times t\\)), where \\(t\\) is the total round-trip travel time of the reflected wave pulse." },
                { front: "Why do echo and sonar formulas divide by 2?", back: "Because the emitted pulse must travel to the reflecting object AND travel back to the detector, covering twice the one-way distance (\\(2d = vt\\))." },
                { front: "What happens to wave speed, frequency, and wavelength when a wave enters a new medium?", back: "Frequency remains constant (determined solely by the source). Wave speed changes depending on the medium, causing wavelength to change proportionally (\\(v \\propto \\lambda\\))." },
                { front: "Calculation: A sound wave has frequency 500 Hz and travels at 340 m/s. What is its wavelength?", back: "\\(\\lambda = \\frac{v}{f} = \\frac{340}{500} = 0.68\\,\\mathrm{m}\\)." },
                { front: "Common Mistake: Forgetting to divide by 2 in echo and sonar problems.", back: "Remember: Multiplying speed by total time gives round-trip distance (to the obstacle and back). You MUST divide by 2 to find the one-way distance to the barrier or seabed." },
                { front: "Common Mistake: Confusing wave frequency with wave period.", back: "Frequency is cycles PER second (Hz), while period is seconds PER cycle (s). If frequency is 50 Hz, period is \\(T = \\frac{1}{50} = 0.02\\,\\mathrm{s}\\), not 50 s." }
            ],
