            flashcards: [
                { front: "What is the physical definition and formula for pressure, and what is its SI unit?", back: "\\(P = \\frac{F}{A}\\). The SI unit is the Pascal (Pa), where \\(1\\,\\mathrm{Pa} = 1\\,\\mathrm{N/m^2}\\)." },
                { front: "What are the standard numerical equivalents of standard atmospheric pressure \\(P_{\\text{atm}}\\) at sea level?", back: "\\(P_{\\text{atm}} = 1.013 \\times 10^5\\,\\mathrm{Pa} = 101.3\\,\\mathrm{kPa} = 760\\,\\mathrm{mmHg} = 1.013\\,\\mathrm{bar}\\)." },
                { front: "What is the formula for the hydrostatic pressure measured by a mercury barometer?", back: "\\(P = \\rho g h\\), where for mercury \\(\\rho = 13{,}600\\,\\mathrm{kg/m^3}\\), giving \\(1.013 \\times 10^5\\,\\mathrm{Pa}\\) at \\(h = 0.76\\,\\mathrm{m}\\)." },
                { front: "What is the formula for enclosed gas pressure measured by an open U-tube manometer?", back: "\\(P_{\\text{gas}} = P_{\\text{atm}} + \\rho g h\\), where \\(h\\) is the excess height of the liquid in the open limb." },
                { front: "What is the formula for total absolute hydrostatic pressure at depth \\(h\\) in a liquid?", back: "\\(P = P_{\\text{atm}} + \\rho g h\\), increasing linearly with depth due to the weight of the fluid column." },
                { front: "What is the formula for the pressure difference between two different depths \\(h_1\\) and \\(h_2\\) in a static liquid?", back: "\\(P_2 - P_1 = \\rho g (h_2 - h_1)\\). It depends solely on vertical elevation difference, independent of container geometry." },
                { front: "State Pascal's Principle and its hydraulic transmission ratio formula.", back: "Pressure applied to an enclosed static liquid is transmitted undiminished in all directions: \\(\\frac{F_1}{A_1} = \\frac{F_2}{A_2} \\implies F_2 = F_1 \\left(\\frac{A_2}{A_1}\\right)\\)." },
                { front: "In a hydraulic lift, how are piston displacements \\(s_1\\) and \\(s_2\\) related to piston areas?", back: "By conservation of incompressible fluid volume: \\(A_1 s_1 = A_2 s_2 \\implies s_2 = s_1 \\left(\\frac{A_1}{A_2}\\right)\\)." },
                { front: "State Archimedes' Principle and give its mathematical equation.", back: "An object immersed in fluid experiences an upward buoyant force equal to the weight of displaced fluid: \\(F_b = \\rho_f g V_{\\text{sub}}\\)." },
                { front: "What is the equilibrium condition for a floating body in terms of densities and volumes?", back: "\\(\\rho_{\\text{obj}} V_{\\text{obj}} = \\rho_f V_{\\text{sub}}\\) (total object weight equals displaced fluid weight)." },
                { front: "What is the formula for the submerged fraction of a floating body?", back: "\\(\\frac{V_{\\text{sub}}}{V_{\\text{obj}}} = \\frac{\\rho_{\\text{obj}}}{\\rho_f} = \\frac{d_{\\text{obj}}}{d_f}\\)." },
                { front: "What is the formula for the apparent weight \\(W_{\\text{app}}\\) of a submerged object measured by a scale?", back: "\\(W_{\\text{app}} = W - F_b = (\\rho_{\\text{obj}} - \\rho_f) V g\\)." },
                { front: "What is relative density (specific gravity) \\(d\\) and what are its units?", back: "\\(d = \\frac{\\rho}{\\rho_{\\text{water}}} = \\frac{m}{m_{\\text{water}}}\\). It is a dimensionless ratio (no units), referenced to water (\\(1000\\,\\mathrm{kg/m^3}\\))." },
                { front: "How is the floating equilibrium condition formulated using relative densities?", back: "\\(d V = d' V'\\), where \\(d\\) and \\(d'\\) are the relative densities of the object and the liquid respectively." },
                { front: "What is specific weight (volume weight) \\(\\varpi\\) and what is its SI unit?", back: "\\(\\varpi = \\frac{W}{V} = \\rho g\\). Its SI unit is Newton per cubic meter (\\(\\mathrm{N/m^3}\\))." },
                { front: "What is the balancing condition for two immiscible liquids in an open U-tube?", back: "\\(\\rho_1 h_1 = \\rho_2 h_2 \\implies \\frac{h_1}{h_2} = \\frac{\\rho_2}{\\rho_1}\\). Balancing column heights are inversely proportional to liquid densities." },
                { front: "What is the formula for the total hydrostatic thrust \\(F_{\\text{thrust}}\\) on a vertical submerged wall or dam?", back: "\\(F_{\\text{thrust}} = P_{\\text{avg}} A = \\frac{1}{2}\\rho g h \\times (w h) = \\frac{1}{2}\\rho g w h^2\\)." },
                { front: "At approximately what depth in freshwater does absolute hydrostatic pressure double atmospheric pressure?", back: "\\(h = \\frac{P_{\\text{atm}}}{\\rho g} = \\frac{101{,}300}{1000 \\times 9.80} \\approx 10.3\\,\\mathrm{m}\\)." },
                { front: "What happens when an object with density \\(\\rho > \\rho_f\\) is placed in a fluid?", back: "Its weight exceeds the maximum buoyant force (\\(W > F_b\\)), so it accelerates downward and sinks to the bottom." },
                { front: "What happens when an object with density \\(\\rho = \\rho_f\\) is placed in a fluid?", back: "\\(W = F_b\\) everywhere, so the object is in neutral equilibrium and remains suspended at any depth." },
                { front: "Does the height of a mercury barometer column depend on the tube's diameter or tilt?", back: "No; vertical column height depends solely on the balance \\(P = \\rho g h\\). Tilting lengthens the mercury path, but the vertical height remains exactly 76 cm." },
                { front: "Common Mistake: Does a heavy steel ship float because steel is lighter than water?", back: "No; steel is denser than water, but the ship's hollow shape gives it a large overall volume, making its average density much less than water." }
            ],
