            flashcards: [
                { front: "What is the physical meaning of electric charge quantization and its formula?", back: "\\(q = n|e|\\). Any observable electric charge is an integer multiple \\(n\\) of the elementary charge \\(|e| = 1.60 \\times 10^{-19}\\,\\mathrm{C}\\)." },
                { front: "What is the formula for the net positive charge of an atomic nucleus?", back: "\\(q_{\\text{nucleus}} = Ze\\), where \\(Z\\) is the atomic number (number of protons) and \\(e = 1.60 \\times 10^{-19}\\,\\mathrm{C}\\)." },
                { front: "What is the formula for charge redistribution when two identical spherical conductors touch?", back: "\\(q_A' = q_B' = \\frac{q_A + q_B}{2}\\). The total net algebraic charge is divided equally between the two identical spheres." },
                { front: "State Coulomb's Law and its mathematical equation for two point charges.", back: "\\(F = k\\frac{|q_1 q_2|}{r^2}\\), where \\(k = 9.0 \\times 10^9\\,\\mathrm{N\\cdot m^2/C^2}\\). The force is attractive for opposite signs and repulsive for like signs." },
                { front: "How does the electrostatic force between two charges change if the distance between them is doubled or tripled?", back: "By the inverse-square law, doubling distance (\\(2r\\)) reduces the force to \\(1/4\\); tripling distance (\\(3r\\)) reduces the force to \\(1/9\\)." },
                { front: "What is the formula for the electric field \\(E\\) created by a point charge \\(q\\) at distance \\(r\\)?", back: "\\(E = k\\frac{|q|}{r^2}\\). The SI unit is Newton per Coulomb (\\(\\mathrm{N/C}\\)) or Volt per meter (\\(\\mathrm{V/m}\\))." },
                { front: "What is the formula for the electrostatic force \\(\\vec{F}\\) experienced by a charge \\(q\\) in an electric field \\(\\vec{E}\\)?", back: "\\(\\vec{F} = q\\vec{E}\\). The force is in the direction of \\(\\vec{E}\\) for positive charges, and opposite to \\(\\vec{E}\\) for negative charges." },
                { front: "What is the formula relating uniform electric field strength \\(E\\), potential difference \\(V\\), and plate separation \\(d\\)?", back: "\\(E = \\frac{V}{d} \\iff V = Ed\\)." },
                { front: "State the principle of Superposition for electrostatic forces.", back: "The net electrostatic force on any charge is the vector sum of all individual forces exerted on it by other charges: \\(\\vec{F}_{\\text{net}} = \\sum \\vec{F}_i\\)." },
                { front: "What are the three fundamental methods of charging (electrification)?", back: "1. Friction (rubbing transfers electrons), 2. Conduction/Contact (direct charge sharing), 3. Induction (charge polarization without touching)." },
                { front: "Where does excess electric charge reside and concentrate on an isolated conductor?", back: "Excess charge resides exclusively on the outer surface and concentrates most densely at sharp corners and pointed tips." },
                { front: "What is an electroscope and how does it detect electric charge?", back: "An instrument with delicate conducting metal leaves that diverge when like charges spread across them due to electrostatic repulsion." },
                { front: "What is the charge and mass of an electron?", back: "Charge \\(-e = -1.60 \\times 10^{-19}\\,\\mathrm{C}\\), rest mass \\(m_e = 9.11 \\times 10^{-31}\\,\\mathrm{kg}\\)." },
                { front: "What is an electric field line and in what direction do field lines point?", back: "Lines indicating the direction of force on a positive test charge: they diverge radially outward from positive charges and terminate on negative charges." },
                { front: "Can two electric field lines ever cross each other? Explain why.", back: "No; if they crossed, the electric field at the intersection point would have two different directions simultaneously, which is physically impossible." },
                { front: "What is an alpha particle and what is its net electric charge?", back: "The nucleus of a helium atom (2 protons and 2 neutrons), carrying a charge of \\(q_\\alpha = +2e = +3.20 \\times 10^{-19}\\,\\mathrm{C}\\)." },
                { front: "What is a Faraday cage and how does it work?", back: "A hollow metallic enclosure that shields its interior from external static electric fields by redistributing surface charges so \\(E = 0\\) inside." },
                { front: "How is the acceleration of a charged particle in an electric field calculated?", back: "\\(a = \\frac{F}{m} = \\frac{qE}{m}\\)." },
                { front: "Why do neutral objects get attracted to a charged balloon or comb?", back: "Through electrostatic induction: the charged object polarizes the neutral atoms, pulling opposite charges closer than like charges, producing a net attraction." },
                { front: "What is Coulomb's constant \\(k\\) expressed in terms of vacuum permittivity \\(\\varepsilon_0\\)?", back: "\\(k = \\frac{1}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0} \\approx 8.99 \\times 10^9\\,\\mathrm{N\\cdot m^2/C^2}\\)." },
                { front: "What happens when an uncharged metal sphere is touched by a charged sphere of identical radius?", back: "The total charge is shared equally between the two spheres, each ending up with exactly half of the initial net charge." },
                { front: "Common Mistake: Does mass increase when an object is charged positively?", back: "No; an object becomes positively charged by LOSING electrons, so its mass slightly decreases (by \\(n \\times m_e\\))." }
            ],
