            flashcards: [
                { front: "What are the four defining characteristics of an ideal fluid?", back: "1. Incompressible (constant density \\(\\rho\\))\n2. Non-viscous (zero internal shear friction)\n3. Steady laminar flow (velocity at any point is constant over time)\n4. Irrotational (zero angular circulation)." },
                { front: "What is volume flow rate and what are its formulas and SI unit?", back: "\\(Q_V = \\frac{\\Delta V}{\\Delta t} = A v\\). It is the volume of fluid passing a cross-section per second. SI unit: cubic meter per second (\\(\\mathrm{m^3/s}\\)). (Note: \\(1\\,\\mathrm{m^3/s} = 1000\\,\\mathrm{L/s}\\))." },
                { front: "What is mass flow rate and its relation to volume flow rate?", back: "\\(Q_m = \\frac{\\Delta m}{\\Delta t} = \\rho Q_V = \\rho A v\\). SI unit: kilogram per second (\\(\\mathrm{kg/s}\\))." },
                { front: "State the Equation of Continuity for incompressible fluid flow.", back: "\\(A_1 v_1 = A_2 v_2 = Q_V = \\text{constant}\\). When pipe area decreases, fluid flow speed increases proportionally to conserve mass." },
                { front: "If the diameter of a pipe is halved, how does the flow velocity change?", back: "Cross-sectional area \\(A = \\frac{\\pi d^2}{4}\\) decreases to \\(1/4\\), so by continuity \\(v_2 = 4 v_1\\) (flow speed quadruples)." },
                { front: "State Bernoulli's General Equation and identify each term.", back: "\\(P + \\frac{1}{2}\\rho v^2 + \\rho gz = \\text{constant}\\), where \\(P\\) is static pressure, \\(\\frac{1}{2}\\rho v^2\\) is dynamic pressure, and \\(\\rho gz\\) is hydrostatic potential energy per unit volume." },
                { front: "What is Bernoulli's principle for a horizontal conduit?", back: "\\(P_1 + \\frac{1}{2}\\rho v_1^2 = P_2 + \\frac{1}{2}\\rho v_2^2\\). In regions where flow speed increases, static pressure must decrease." },
                { front: "State Torricelli's Law for liquid efflux from an orifice.", back: "\\(v = \\sqrt{2gh}\\), where \\(h\\) is the depth of the orifice below the open surface of the container. The efflux speed equals the speed gained by a free-falling particle from height \\(h\\)." },
                { front: "How does a Venturi meter measure fluid flow speed?", back: "A constriction reduces flow area (\\(A_2 < A_1\\)), increasing speed (\\(v_2 > v_1\\)) and creating a measurable pressure drop \\(\\Delta P = \\frac{1}{2}\\rho v_1^2\\left[\\left(\\frac{A_1}{A_2}\\right)^2 - 1\\right]\\)." },
                { front: "Define dynamic viscosity \\(\\eta\\) and its SI unit.", back: "Dynamic viscosity measures internal shear friction resisting fluid deformation and flow. Its SI unit is Pascal-second (\\(\\mathrm{Pa\\cdot s}\\) or \\(\\mathrm{N\\cdot s/m^2} = \\mathrm{kg/(m\\cdot s)}\\)). (\\(1\\,\\mathrm{Pa\\cdot s} = 10\\,\\mathrm{poise}\\))." },
                { front: "State Newton's Law of Viscosity.", back: "\\(F = \\eta A \\frac{\\Delta v}{\\Delta z}\\), where \\(F\\) is the viscous shear force, \\(A\\) is surface area, and \\(\\frac{\\Delta v}{\\Delta z}\\) is the velocity gradient perpendicular to the flow." },
                { front: "Describe the velocity profile of viscous laminar flow in a cylindrical pipe.", back: "Friction at the walls creates a parabolic velocity profile: velocity is zero at the tube boundary (no-slip condition) and maximum at the central axis (\\(v_{\\max}\\)). The average speed is \\(v_{\\text{avg}} = \\frac{v_{\\max}}{2}\\)." },
                { front: "State Poiseuille's Law for laminar volume flow rate through a pipe.", back: "\\(Q_V = \\frac{\\pi R^4 \\Delta P}{8\\eta l}\\). Flow rate is directly proportional to \\(R^4\\) and pressure drop \\(\\Delta P\\), and inversely proportional to viscosity \\(\\eta\\) and pipe length \\(l\\)." },
                { front: "Why is the \\(R^4\\) factor in Poiseuille's law physiologically critical?", back: "A slight reduction in arterial radius drastically restricts blood flow (e.g. a 16% narrowing cuts flow rate by 50% since \\(0.84^4 \\approx 0.50\\)), forcing the heart to increase blood pressure." },
                { front: "State Stokes' Law for viscous drag on a sphere.", back: "\\(F_d = 6\\pi \\eta R v\\), where \\(R\\) is the sphere radius, \\(\\eta\\) is fluid viscosity, and \\(v\\) is speed. It applies to smooth laminar flow around small spheres at low Reynolds numbers." },
                { front: "What three forces act on a sphere falling in a viscous fluid at terminal velocity?", back: "1. Downward gravitational weight: \\(W = \\frac{4}{3}\\pi R^3 \\rho_s g\\)\n2. Upward buoyant force: \\(F_b = \\frac{4}{3}\\pi R^3 \\rho_f g\\)\n3. Upward Stokes drag: \\(F_d = 6\\pi\\eta R v_t\\)." },
                { front: "What is the formula for the terminal settling velocity of a sphere?", back: "\\(v_t = \\frac{2 R^2 (\\rho_s - \\rho_f) g}{9 \\eta}\\), derived from the equilibrium condition \\(W = F_b + F_d\\)." },
                { front: "How does temperature affect the viscosity of liquids versus gases?", back: "In liquids, increasing temperature weakens intermolecular bonds, decreasing viscosity. In gases, increasing temperature increases molecular collisions, increasing viscosity." },
                { front: "How does a Pitot tube determine airspeed?", back: "By measuring the difference between stagnation pressure (at the tube tip where \\(v=0\\)) and static pressure: \\(v = \\sqrt{\\frac{2\\Delta P}{\\rho}}\\)." },
                { front: "Explain how aerodynamic lift is generated by an airplane wing (airfoil).", back: "Curvature forces air over the upper wing surface to travel faster than air beneath it. By Bernoulli's principle, higher speed creates lower pressure on top, generating an upward net lift force." },
                { front: "What is the Magnus Effect?", back: "A spinning ball drags air around itself, speeding up air on one side and slowing it on the opposite side. The resulting Bernoulli pressure differential creates a sideways deflecting force (curveball)." },
                { front: "What is the difference between laminar and turbulent flow?", back: "Laminar flow is smooth, orderly, and streamlined (concentric fluid layers glide without mixing); turbulent flow is chaotic, characterized by eddies, vortices, and high resistance." }
            ],
